.TH GGE\-TEXT 7 "generalGameEngine" "Engine Manual" "Engine Manual"
.SH NAME
gge\-text \- fonts, drawing and measuring strings, layout
.SH DESCRIPTION
text.h draws strings with TrueType fonts loaded from assets/, falling back to the built\-in 8x8 font whenever a real font is unavailable, so text code works from the first build. Fonts need the engine built with ENGINE_WITH_TTF (gge\-build(7)) and \fBEngineConfig.text = true\fR; without either, every Font is the debug font and the calls still work.
.PP
.SH FONTS
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
static Font ui, big;
static void on_enter(Engine *e, Scene *s) {
  ui  = text_load_font("fonts/pixel.ttf", 8);    /* point size in logical pixels */
  big = text_load_font("fonts/pixel.ttf", 24);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fBFont\fR is a small value: load once per size you use (each size is a separate font), keep it in a static, copy it freely, never free it. A failed load logs and returns a zero Font, which draws with the 8x8 font; \fB(Font){0}\fR is therefore a valid "no font" everywhere, and the menu, dialog and cutscene modules accept it. Sizes are in logical pixels: a 12\-point font on a 320x180 canvas is 12 of those 180 pixels tall, whatever the window size. For pixel fonts use the size the font was designed at (often 8 or 16) so glyphs land on the pixel grid.
.PP
.SH DRAWING
.TP
\fBtext_draw(f, x, y, color, s)\fR
one line, top\-left at x,y. No wrapping, no newlines.
.TP
\fBtext_size(f, s, &w, &h)\fR
pixel size of s.
.TP
\fBtext_line_height(f)\fR
height of a line, for stacking.
.TP
\fBtext_draw_debug(x, y, color, s) / draw_debug_text\fR
the 8x8 font, always.
Rendered strings are cached per font, color and string (64 entries), so labels that do not change are free to draw every frame, and a score that changes now and then costs one small texture per change. A string that changes every frame (a millisecond timer) re\-renders every frame; that is fine for a few of them.
.PP
.SH LAYOUT
Everything is arithmetic on text_size:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
static void draw_centered(Font f, float cx, float y, Color c, const char *s) {
  int w, h; text_size(f, s, &w, &h);
  text_draw(f, cx \- w / 2.0f, y, c, s);
}
static void draw_right(Font f, float right, float y, Color c, const char *s) {
  int w, h; text_size(f, s, &w, &h);
  text_draw(f, right \- w, y, c, s);
}
/* several lines */
float y = 40;
for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) { text_draw(ui, 20, y, COLOR_WHITE, lines[i]); y += text_line_height(ui) + 2; }
/* a label with a value */
char buf[32]; snprintf(buf, sizeof buf, "Score %d", g\->score);
text_draw(ui, 8, 8, COLOR_YELLOW, buf);
.EE
.RE
.PP
A drop shadow is the same string drawn once in black at (x+1, y+1) and once in the color on top. An outline is four offsets. A panel behind text is draw_rect with a translucent color, sized from text_size plus padding.
.PP
.SH WORD WRAP
The dialog module wraps for you (gge\-dialog(7)). For your own paragraphs, wrap by measuring: accumulate words into a line, measure the candidate with text_size, and start a new line when it exceeds the width:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
static int wrap(Font f, const char *text, float max_w, char out[][128], int max_lines) {
  int lines = 0; char line[128] = ""; const char *p = text;
  while (*p && lines < max_lines) {
    const char *end = p; while (*end && *end != ' ') ++end;
    char word[64], trial[128]; snprintf(word, sizeof word, "%.*s", (int)(end \- p), p);
    snprintf(trial, sizeof trial, "%s%s%s", line, *line ? " " : "", word);
    int w, h; text_size(f, trial, &w, &h);
    if ((float)w > max_w && *line) { snprintf(out[lines++], 128, "%s", line); snprintf(line, sizeof line, "%s", word); }
    else snprintf(line, sizeof line, "%s", trial);
    p = *end ? end + 1 : end;
  }
  if (*line && lines < max_lines) snprintf(out[lines++], 128, "%s", line);
  return lines;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Wrap once when the text changes (in update or on_enter), not every frame.
.PP
.SH TYPEWRITER TEXT
Draw a prefix of the string that grows with time: \fBsnprintf(shown, sizeof shown, "%.*s", (int)chars, text)\fR with \fBchars += speed * dt\fR in update. Each new prefix is a new cache entry, which is fine for one dialog box. The dialog module implements exactly this.
.PP
.SH NUMBERS THAT CHANGE EVERY FRAME
A frame counter, a stopwatch or an fps display changes every frame and defeats the cache. Options: use draw_debug_text for it (no cache, cheap); update the string only every few ticks; or draw digits from a sprite sheet of numerals, which is also how arcade games get outlined, styled numbers.
.PP
.SH THE DEBUG FONT
Always available, 8x8 pixels per character, no file. Right for overlays, the F3 layer (debug_text(3)) and for placeholder UI until fonts exist. Its size is fixed, so at a 640x360 canvas it is small and at 320x180 it is large; if the game is pixel\-art at a low resolution, an 8 px TrueType pixel font gives the same look with a real character set.
.PP
.SH CHARACTER SETS
TrueType fonts draw whatever glyphs they contain; strings are UTF\-8. The debug font covers printable ASCII. Typed text from engine_text_input(3) arrives as UTF\-8 too, so a name entered with accented letters draws correctly with a font that has them.
.PP
.SH PITFALLS
.IP \(bu 2
Loading fonts every frame: each load is a new font and a new cache entry; load in on_enter.
.IP \(bu 2
Passing a stack buffer that goes out of scope: text_draw copies the string into its cache, so this is safe; menu labels and dialog lines are kept by pointer and are not (gge\-menu(7), gge\-dialog(7)).
.IP \(bu 2
Measuring with the wrong font: text_size and text_draw must get the same Font.
.IP \(bu 2
Fractional positions: text drawn at x = 10.5 lands between logical pixels and blurs at integer window scales; round UI positions.
